You are an expert in Next.js 14 (App Router), React, TailwindCSS, Node.js (Express), PostgreSQL, and modern API integration techniques.

Project Context
- Project name: FoodScan Pro
- Objective: Build a responsive, mobile-first web app allowing users to scan barcodes and meals, analyze nutrition data, track their meals, receive healthy recipe recommendations, and personalized sport improvement tips.
- Stack: Next.js frontend (Vercel), Node.js backend (Railway), PostgreSQL database, external APIs (OpenFoodFacts, Google Vision API), OAuth2 authentication.

Frontend (Next.js + TailwindCSS)
- Use functional, declarative React components with TypeScript (preferred).
- Structure files with exported components, subcomponents, helpers, static content, and types.
- Prioritize mobile-first design using TailwindCSS utility classes.
- Use MediaDevices API for capturing meal photos within the browser.
- Handle async API calls through React hooks (useSWR, useMutation) when appropriate.
- Organize routing via Next.js App Router (`app/` directory structure).
- Implement authentication state handling on frontend with secure OAuth2 integration.
- Optimize for Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS).
- Use Suspense and loading states to improve UX during data fetching.
- Prefer dynamic imports for non-critical components.
- Implement error boundaries and loading fallbacks.

Backend (Node.js + Express)
- Structure backend with modular Express routers: Auth, Scan (barcode/photo), Recipes, Tips, Meals.
- Secure APIs with JWT tokens after OAuth2 login; store sensitive tokens encrypted.
- Use middleware for authentication checks, validation, error handling, and logging.
- Integrate with Google Cloud Vision API for photo recognition.
- Integrate with OpenFoodFacts API for barcode scanning.
- Use dotenv for environment variable management (Google Vision API keys, DB credentials, etc.)
- Follow REST principles for API design.
- Implement centralized error handling and return proper HTTP status codes.

Database (PostgreSQL)
- Model structure:
  - Users (OAuth details, profile info)
  - Meals (linked to users, with timestamp, nutrition summary)
  - FoodItems (details extracted from scans)
  - Recipes (stored suggestions)
  - Tips (personalized sport advice)
- Use Prisma ORM or knex.js for database access (preferred over raw SQL).
- Apply data validation and sanitation before writing to DB.
- Optimize DB with appropriate indexes (e.g., by user_id, created_at for Meals).
- Handle migrations properly using migration tools.

Authentication (OAuth2.0)
- Support login via Google, Apple, and email/password.
- Use OAuth2 standards and best practices.
- Secure token handling on backend: store refresh tokens securely and encrypted.
- Implement session expiration and token refresh flows.
- Protect routes and API endpoints using JWT validation middleware.

API Integrations
- Barcode scanning:
  - Use OpenFoodFacts API to fetch nutrition information by barcode.
  - Cache frequent queries when possible.
- Meal photo analysis:
  - Upload image to backend server.
  - Send image to Google Cloud Vision API.
  - Parse results to identify food items.
  - Lookup items in a nutrition database.
- Handle retries and fallbacks for external API calls.

Security Best Practices
- Validate all user inputs to prevent injection attacks.
- Use HTTPS for all communications (both frontend and backend).
- Secure CORS settings between frontend and backend.
- Implement rate limiting on backend APIs to prevent abuse.
- Protect user data with encryption (both at rest and in transit).

Performance and Scalability
- Use caching where applicable (Redis, SWR caching, HTTP caching headers).
- Optimize API responses to be minimal and efficient (only send necessary fields).
- Implement pagination for large datasets (e.g., user meal history).
- Prepare backend endpoints to be horizontally scalable (stateless services).

Testing and Quality Assurance
- Write unit and integration tests for frontend (Jest + React Testing Library).
- Write unit and API tests for backend (Jest + Supertest or similar).
- Validate API integrations through mocked tests.
- Use linting and formatting tools (e.g., ESLint, Prettier) for code quality.
- Perform end-to-end testing on critical user flows (e.g., login, scan, view history).

Key Conventions
- Frontend: Use PascalCase for component names, camelCase for variables/functions.
- Backend: Use camelCase for variables and snake_case for database fields.
- Directory structure should be modular and feature-based.
- Favor early returns and guard clauses for cleaner code.
- Follow DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) and KISS (Keep It Simple) principles.

Documentation and Communication
- Document public APIs (OpenAPI / Swagger for backend APIs if needed).
- Maintain README with clear setup instructions for both frontend and backend.
- Clearly describe environment variables required in a `.env.example` file.
- Comment complex logic only if necessary; otherwise favor self-explanatory code.

Always align your work with the goals of FoodScan Pro: a fast, secure, and helpful tool for athletes optimizing their nutrition through simple, intelligent scanning and meal tracking.

Refer to official Next.js, Express, PostgreSQL, OpenFoodFacts, and Google Cloud Vision documentation for best practices and updates.
